{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothRecursionArith","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.RothRecursionArith","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Floor.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Floor.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Rat.Floor","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.BigOperators.Group.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Intervals","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:RothArith.sq_mul_le_two_mul_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"RothArith.sq_mul_le_two_mul_witness","kind":"theorem","line":251,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRecursionArith.lean#L251-L255","doc":"**Satisfiability at that instance.**  With `θ = 1`, `r = 1`, `n = 3`, `S = 1` every\nhypothesis holds and the conclusion reads `1 ≤ 2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:RothArith.sum_max_truncation_active","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"RothArith.sum_max_truncation_active","kind":"theorem","line":240,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRecursionArith.lean#L240-L249","doc":"**The truncation is doing work.**  At `θ = 1`, `r = 1`, `n = 3` the truncated sum is `1`\n(terms `1, 0, 0`) while the untruncated sum `∑ (θ − ν/r)` is `0` (terms `1, 0, −1`).\n\nThey differ, so no reading of the theorem in which `max 0` is dropped is the same theorem —\nand the untruncated version is the *smaller* quantity, which is the wrong direction for a\nlower bound on a sum being estimated from above. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:RothArith.le_two_mul_max_of_sum_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"RothArith.le_two_mul_max_of_sum_le","kind":"theorem","line":200,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRecursionArith.lean#L200-L232","doc":"**Mahler §13, combined — and it needs no side condition.**\n\n    θ ≤ 2 · max (S/n, q)        for any `q` with `S ≤ q²·r`.\n\nThe case split is on whether the cut-off `θ·r` falls inside the range of columns:\n\n* `(n−1)/r ≤ θ` — it does not, every column contributes, and `le_two_mul_div_of_sum_le` gives\n  `θ ≤ 2S/n`;\n* otherwise `θ·r < n−1 ≤ n`, which is exactly `sq_mul_le_two_mul_of_sum_le`'s hypothesis, and\n  that gives `θ² ≤ 2q² ≤ (2q)²`, hence `θ ≤ 2q`.\n\n⚠ Note what is **absent**: no hypothesis relating `S`, `r` and `n`.  The two branches are\ncomplementary by construction, so the combination is unconditional.  An earlier form of this\ndemanded `S·r ≤ n` in order to force the second branch — see the ninth correction; that\ncondition is both unnecessary and false in the intended regime. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:RothArith.sq_branch_strictly_sharper","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"RothArith.sq_branch_strictly_sharper","kind":"theorem","line":189,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRecursionArith.lean#L189-L198","doc":"⚠ **The sharpening bites.**  At `r = S = 1` the squared branch bounds `θ` by `√2` and the\nrounded one by `2q = 2`.  `θ = 3/2` lies between them: the rounded branch admits it, the\nsquared branch does not.\n\nSo `sq_le_two_mul_max_of_sum_le` is strictly stronger than `le_two_mul_max_of_sum_le`'s second\ncase rather than a restatement of it — which is the whole point, since the factor recovered\nhere is the one that separates this arc's constant from §14's. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:RothArith.sq_le_two_mul_max_of_sum_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"RothArith.sq_le_two_mul_max_of_sum_le","kind":"theorem","line":164,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRecursionArith.lean#L164-L187","doc":"**The sharp form: case 2 kept squared, with no `√2` lost.**\n\n`le_two_mul_max_of_sum_le` below turns case 2's `θ²·r ≤ 2S` into `θ ≤ 2q` via `S ≤ q²·r`, and\nthat step costs a factor `√2`: what `θ² ≤ 2q²` gives is `θ ≤ √2·q`, not `θ ≤ 2q`.\n\n⚠ **That loss is not in the source.**  §13's display (2) reads\n`J(A) ≤ t + √((2/r_m)·J(W)) ≤ t + 2·√(J(W)/n)`, and the `2` on the right is `√4`, arriving\nthrough `2/r_m ≤ 4/n` — never through `√2 ≤ 2`.  This arc's module header had recorded \"the\nweakening of `√2` to `2`\" as *confirmed against the source*; it is the arc's own, and it is\navoidable.\n\nKeeping the disjunction is what makes it avoidable: the two cases are exactly display (2)'s two\nbranches, and a caller can carry the square through `RothLemmaStatement.radicand_weaken`\ninstead of taking a root here.  The proof is the same as `le_two_mul_max_of_sum_le`'s, stopping\none step earlier in each branch. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:RothArith.le_two_mul_div_of_sum_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"RothArith.le_two_mul_div_of_sum_le","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRecursionArith.lean#L131-L162","doc":"**Mahler §13, case (1) — the SHARP form.**\n\nWhen the cut-off falls outside the range (`(n−1)/r ≤ θ`, so every one of the `n` terms is\nunclipped), Gauss's sum eats half of them and the conclusion carries a factor `2/n`:\n\n    θ ≤ 2S/n.\n\n⚠ The `2/n` is the entire content.  Dropping it — keeping only the `ν = 0` term, which gives\n`θ ≤ S` unconditionally — is true but useless in the application, where `S` is of size `n·Φ`.\n`2S/n` is then of size `Φ`, and `S` is of size `n·Φ`; the factor separating them is the matrix\nsize.  See the ninth correction in `QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:RothArith.sq_mul_le_two_mul_of_sum_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"RothArith.sq_mul_le_two_mul_of_sum_le","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRecursionArith.lean#L87-L129","doc":"**The quadratic bound — Mahler §13's second case.**\n\nIf the truncated sum `∑_{ν<n} max(0, θ − ν/r)` is at most `S`, and there are enough terms\n(`θ·r ≤ n`), then\n\n    θ² · r ≤ 2 · S,\n\ni.e. `θ ≤ √(2S/r)`.  Stated squared so that no `Real.sqrt` is needed and everything stays in\n`ℚ`.\n\nThe cut-off is `K = ⌈θ·r⌉`: it is at most `n` by hypothesis, at least `θ·r` by the ceiling\nproperty, and at most `θ·r + 1` — which is exactly what makes the arithmetic progression's\ncorrection term `K(K−1)/(2r)` at most half of `K·θ`, leaving `K·θ/2 ≥ θ²r/2`.\n\n⚠ This is the shape a linear combination cannot produce, and its absence is what the sixth\ncorrection was detecting. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:RothArith.sum_max_sub_ge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"RothArith.sum_max_sub_ge","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRecursionArith.lean#L64-L85","doc":"**The truncated sum bound.**  For any cut-off `K ≤ n`, the sum of `max 0 (θ − ν/r)` over\n`ν < n` is at least the untruncated arithmetic progression over `ν < K`.\n\nTwo slacknesses, both free: the tail `K ≤ ν < n` is dropped because every term is\nnon-negative, and `max 0 x ≥ x` is used on the retained terms.  No hypothesis relating `K` to\n`θ` or `r` is needed — the caller chooses `K` to make the bound sharp. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:RothArith.sum_range_div","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"RothArith.sum_range_div","kind":"theorem","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRecursionArith.lean#L51-L62","doc":"Gauss's sum, divided through. ","signatureChunk":0}]}